if you look at the sky  after sunset and you see something very  bright glowing near the horizon and you  say oh my god what's that it's almost  certain to be venus it's one of the  brightest objects in the sky  we call it the evening star or the  morning star because it tends to because  it's close to to the sun in the solar  system it tends to follow the sun or or  precede it in sunrise  so the symbol for venus is actually a  hand mirror  because venus was the roman goddess of  love and beauty and we see this symbol  all over the place not just in astronomy  because it's culturally it's now our  symbol for woman or for  femininity  and this is counterbalanced by the  symbol for mars which people recognize  as a symbol for for men it's a it's a  circle with a  an arrow coming out of it and that's the  the  um representing the shield and the spear  because mars was the roman god of war  and so these are these symbols that have  come out of astronomy into everyday life  meaning male and female well it's it's  often called our sister planet because  it's right next door to earth it's the  next planet closest to the sun  and it's also almost the same size as  earth  which is interesting and it has an  atmosphere like earth does as well but  it's not a place that you would want to  go on holiday  not at all because that atmosphere  is actually almost entirely carbon  dioxide and so what we see on venus  is actually a runaway greenhouse effect  so that atmosphere is very very good at  trapping the heat from the sun and  keeping it there so venus is incredibly  hot  on the surface of venus  the temperatures are high enough to melt  lead  and with that is incredible atmospheric  pressure  so standing on the surface of venus you  would feel the same amount of pressure  as if you were 800 meters below the  surface of the  sea  above that if that's not enough to put  off your holiday plans  it also the atmosphere rains sulfuric  acid so basically it's not a very  hospitable place to be even though it's  one of the closer planets to us it's  very unlikely that we're probably going  to send  human beings there in the near or even  in the far future  now in an unexplored region of space  mariner was placed on its mercury  intercept course by the gravity of venus  so it's hard for us to see the surface  of venus or it's impossible for us  because it has this really really thick  atmosphere so we've actually we being  humanity  mostly the americans and the former  soviet union have sent a number of  probes to venus all the way back from  the 60s through the 70s  some of those  orbited and used radar to pierce the  clouds and some of them even managed to  land and now this was a very difficult  piece of engineering to accomplish  because the conditions there are so  harsh and in fact the earlier landers  didn't make it very far they were  crushed by the incredible  temperatures pressures or melted by the  temperatures  but some of them did actually make it  down there so we we do actually have  pictures  of the surface of venus  nasa's mariner spacecraft after  traveling four months and 217 million  miles began fulfilling its mission as it  rendezvoused with the planet venus  this year being 2009 is of course the  400th anniversary of galileo taking a  telescope  and  pointing it at the heavens  and one of the things that he observed  which was very very important  was he managed to observe the full  phases of venus  so venus like the moon  undergoes phases from our point of view  because it changes its position relative  to the sun  and in at galileo's time it was still  working under this  idea of the universe that the earth was  at the center  and his observations of venus undergoing  this full spectrum of phases  didn't fit in with that picture at all  and in fact that was one of the greatest  pieces of evidence that they had  that  that system was wrong and that we had to  move our understanding of the universe  away from the earth being the center  towards the sun being the center  so for me that observation of venus  it just goes to show what astronomy does  astronomy makes us change the way  we see ourselves  as part of the larger universe and  simply by taking a telescope and  pointing it at the heavens and seeing  something new that didn't fit we had to  change our entire world view
 

